# Primary domain: portal (root) + api (/v1) — same origin, so browser->api needs
# no CORS. The gateway and Keycloak each get their OWN subdomain because both
# serve root-level paths that a path-prefix would break: Keycloak's admin/asset
# relative paths, and the gateway's RFC 9728 metadata (it advertises
# <resource>/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource and serves /mcp + the
# well-known at its origin ROOT — under a /mcp path prefix the discovery URL
# would 404 and MCP OAuth could not complete).
# The api serves THREE path spaces, not one, and every one of them needs its own
# matcher here: a path that matches none of them falls through to the portal's
# SPA fallback, which answers 200 with index.html for any path containing no dot
# (cmd/portal/main.go), so a missing route reads as a working endpoint serving
# HTML, not as a 404. /healthz is deliberately NOT routed to the api: the portal
# serves its own, each container is probed on its own port inside the compose
# network, and a public /healthz answering for the api rather than for the thing
# the browser talks to would be the misleading one. Derived and gated by
# internal/deploy.TestApexRoutesEveryAPIPathToTheAPI, which reads the route table
# out of internal/api's own source (audit A11).
{$ORBEAT_DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy /v1/*         api:8080
	reverse_proxy /scim/v2/*    api:8080
	reverse_proxy /openapi.yaml api:8080
	reverse_proxy *             portal:8081
}

auth.{$ORBEAT_DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy keycloak:8080
}

mcp.{$ORBEAT_DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy gateway:8090
}
